However, $BD = DE = EC = t$, so $BD \cdot EC = t^2$. 
Then we have $\frac{\sin(x+y)\sin(y+z)}{\sin x \sin z} = \frac{\sin(x+y)\sin(y+z) AD \cdot AE}{t^2 \sin \alpha \sin \gamma}$. 

Consider the case $x=y=z$. Then we want to find $\frac{\sin(2x)\sin(2x)}{\sin x \sin x} = \frac{\sin^2(2x)}{\sin^2 x} = \frac{4\sin^2 x \cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} = 4\cos^2 x$. 
