\end{equation*}

\textbf{Solution:}

We are rolling six 6-sided dice. We want to find the probability that exactly one die shows a 1 and exactly one die shows a 2. The other four dice can show any number from 3 to 6 (inclusive).

Total number of outcomes: Since each die has 6 possible outcomes, the total number of possible outcomes when rolling six dice is $6^6 = 46656$.

Now we want to find the number of ways to have exactly one 1 and exactly one 2.
First, we choose 1 position for the 1 die out of the 6 dice. This can be done in $\binom{6}{1} = 6$ ways.
Next, we choose 1 position for the 2 die out of the remaining 5 dice. This can be done in $\binom{5}{1} = 5$ ways.
Now we have 4 dice left, and each of these dice can show any number from 3 to 6. So each of these dice has 4 possible outcomes. Thus, the number of ways to have the remaining 4 dice show numbers from 3 to 6 is $4^4 = 256$.

So, the number of ways to have exactly one 1 and exactly one 2 is $\binom{6}{1} \times \binom{5}{1} \times 4^4 = 6 \times 5 \times 256 = 30 \times 256 = 7680$.

The probability of having exactly one 1 and exactly one 2 is
$$P = \frac{\text{Number of ways to have exactly one 1 and exactly one 2}}{\text{Total number of outcomes}} = \frac{7680}{46656} = \frac{3840}{23328} = \frac{1920}{11664} = \frac{960}{5832} = \frac{480}{2916} = \frac{240}{1458} = \frac{120}{729} = \frac{40}{243}$$

The probability is $\frac{7680}{46656} = \frac{40}{243}$.

\boxed{\frac{40}{243}}
\end{equation*}
